{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":599.6500244140625,"text":"of tools internally and have now exposed this as a product called Bell Smith. This is what we're doing in coding, and I think it's, again, a sign of what's going to come in other parts of knowledge work. So what we were able to do is look at the GitHub repo as a centralized way of seeing what work has already been done and what work is continuing to happen. And in particular, GitHub repos are comprised of PRs, or pull requests, which have a GitHub issue and a resolution. And so what we can do is unwind the clock and give those same tasks to agents to see if they could have equivalently done the work of humans. And in doing that, we found a much clearer understanding of which models were overkill, were way too expensive and did not need to be used, and then which ones were actually capable of getting the task done. And I think, again, this is another place where the intuitions that a lot of people have may not be what's actually borne out in practice. So I think you may see we're using a lot of closed source models. We should try and cut costs and use our own self-hosted open source model. You can see the comparison here we have between GPT 5.6 Sol and Kimi K3, and we see that 5.6 Sol is better. It's going 2% better on this internal eval. It's also considerably cheaper than hosting it through a very inference-optimized stack. So it's $2.50 instead of $11, and it's faster half the time. And so there's no real reason why this kind of intuitive sense of using open source would be a legitimate way to drive down costs. And so this has resulted in two main differences. One, we can actually figure out which tools we're going to procure, what the usage limits of those things should be across the organization. But then also we can give recommendations for engineers and say we think that for this GitHub issue, you should actually use this particular coding tool with this model. And this is able to drive the cost down considerably. I think we're spending 90% less, but still able to get the same output. I think the question I would have for you all, we're continuing to expand this approach and take this model of coding to other domains, knowledge work, legal, finance, things like that. But the question is, where are you tracking the history of work that has already been done by that firm? Some of these law firms have been around for like 100 years. And so where is that, I think, quality of the organization stored? Where are you tracking the standard of excellence? And so that today may exist in places like email from the history of responses that people have had with their clients, may exist in SharePoints, may exist in Databricks dumps. But I think this is the key treasure trove that a lot of companies have and don't realize will be the major unlock for evals. And then the unlock for AI adoption more systematically. Once these evals are constructed, they can enable adoption to happen. But we've also seen that there's more advantageous ways that they can be used. So first is right sizing. So rather than just token maximizing, how do you figure out what the right usage limit should be across the organization? Negotiation. Once you realize you have actual leverage, you can take that to the conversation you have in bulk pricing. And then routing. This is becoming a core primitive in a lot of AI products. But can also be done by users directly. And so they can build intelligent routers that actually figure out the right model to use. And then fine tuning or post training. This can be the most reliable way for you to actually mint the tasks and then have an external eval set to make sure that this approach is working. So what I thought I'd leave you with is I think for a lot of companies, they have a competitive advantage that has existed in the heads of employees through things like institutional knowledge. And what evals allow you to do is actually codify the competitive advantage and put that down in words or in scripts, in language. And that will actually be the biggest unlock for making sure that a company succeeds at the agentic era. Okay. That's all. Thanks, everyone. Thank you. Thank you.  Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye.  Bye-bye. Bye-bye.  Bye-bye. Bye-bye.  Bye-bye. Bye-bye.  Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye. Bye-bye.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":4.639999866485596,"text":" of tools internally and have now exposed this as a product called Bell Smith.","tokens":[50364,220,2670,3873,19501,293,362,586,9495,341,382,257,1674,1219,11485,8538,13,50596],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3351326882839203,"compression_ratio":1.7230769395828247,"no_speech_prob":0.3095039427280426},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":4.639999866485596,"end":7.960000038146973,"text":" This is what we're doing in coding, and I think it's, again, a sign of what's going","tokens":[50596,639,307,437,321,434,884,294,17720,11,293,286,519,309,311,11,797,11,257,1465,295,437,311,516,50762],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3351326882839203,"compression_ratio":1.7230769395828247,"no_speech_prob":0.3095039427280426},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":7.960000038146973,"end":9.800000190734863,"text":" to come in other parts of knowledge work.","tokens":[50762,281,808,294,661,3166,295,3601,589,13,50854],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3351326882839203,"compression_ratio":1.7230769395828247,"no_speech_prob":0.3095039427280426},{"id":3,"seek":0,"start":9.800000190734863,"end":14.84000015258789,"text":" So what we were able to do is look at the GitHub repo as a centralized way of seeing","tokens":[50854,407,437,321,645,1075,281,360,307,574,412,264,23331,49040,382,257,32395,636,295,2577,51106],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3351326882839203,"compression_ratio":1.7230769395828247,"no_speech_prob":0.3095039427280426},{"id":4,"seek":0,"start":14.84000015258789,"end":18.84000015258789,"text":" what work